# PIT Tag Mobile App - Cursor Rules

## Project Context

- React Native app with Expo (managed workflow)
- Offline-first BLE tag scanner for fish & wildlife field staff
- TypeScript + React Native Paper + SQLite
- Target: Both iOS and Android

## Essential Code Standards

### TypeScript

- Use strict mode. No `any` types except in documented edge cases.
- All functions must have return type annotations.
- Use interfaces for data models, types for unions/intersections.

### React Patterns

- Functional components only. No class components.
- Use hooks for all state and side effects.
- Custom hooks should start with `use` (e.g., `useScans()`, `useBLE()`).

### File Structure

- Screens go in `/src/screens/`
- Components in `/src/components/`
- Utils in `/src/utils/`
- Contexts in `/src/context/`
- Types in `/src/models/`
- Never create files outside this structure without asking.

## Workflow

- Provide a step by step approach to implementing each feature, solution or fix so I am able to learn as I code this application.

## Field-First UX Rules

### Touch Targets

- All buttons, touchable elements minimum 44x44 pixels.
- Consider field use: gloves, sunlight, wet conditions.

### Offline-First

- Every feature must work without internet.
- Network calls are enhancements, not requirements.
- Never assume connectivity.

### Data Safety

- Confirm all destructive actions (delete, clear, bulk operations).
- Auto-save form state to prevent data loss.
- Show save confirmation to user.

## BLE-Specific Rules

### Error Handling

- Wrap all BLE operations in try/catch.
- Disconnections are expected, not errors - handle gracefully.
- Provide user-friendly error messages, no technical jargon.

### Connection Status

- Show BLE connection status on every relevant screen.
- Use consistent status indicator (icon/badge in header).

### Graceful Degradation

- If BLE fails, allow manual tag ID entry.
- Never block core functionality due to BLE issues.

## Database & Data Integrity

### IDs and Timestamps

- Use UUIDs (uuid library) for all primary keys, not auto-increment.
- Every record needs `createdAt` and `updatedAt` in ISO 8601 format.
- Use UTC for all timestamps, convert to local only for display.

### Database Layer

- All SQLite operations go through `src/utils/database.ts`.
- No raw SQL in components or contexts.
- Export typed functions (e.g., `createScan(data: TagScan)` not raw queries).

### Validation

- Validate data before saving to database.
- Tag ID is required. Other fields are optional unless specified.
- Show validation errors to user before save attempt.

### CSV Export

- Escape commas, quotes, newlines in all text fields.
- Use consistent date format (ISO 8601).
- Test with messy data (special characters, long text).

## Performance

### Lists

- Use FlatList for any list with >20 items (never ScrollView).
- Implement `keyExtractor` and `getItemLayout` when possible.

### Search & Filter

- Debounce search inputs (300ms minimum).
- Show loading indicator during search/filter operations.

## Architecture

### Context Organization

- Each context manages one domain (ScanContext, BLEContext, AppContext).
- No cross-domain logic in contexts.
- Export custom hooks (e.g., `export const useScans = () => useContext(ScanContext)`).

### Component Responsibility

- Components render UI and handle user input only.
- Business logic lives in contexts or utils.
- Keep components under 200 lines - split if larger.

## React Native Paper Usage

- Use Paper components by default (Button, TextInput, Card, List, etc.).
- Import from 'react-native-paper', not base React Native.
- Use Paper theme for colors/spacing, not hardcoded values.
- Only use base React Native components when Paper doesn't provide equivalent.
- Check React Native Paper docs before recommending implemttaions and changes.

## Expo Conventions

- Use Expo APIs when available (expo-location, expo-sqlite, expo-sharing).
- Prefer Expo managed workflow - avoid ejecting.
- Check Expo docs before installing third-party libraries.

## Error Handling Patterns

- Always use try/catch for async operations (database, BLE, file system).
- Log errors to console with context: `console.error('[BLE] Connection failed:', error)`.
- Show user-friendly error in UI: "Unable to connect to reader. Please try again."
- Never crash the app - graceful degradation always.

## Async Patterns

- Use async/await, not .then() chains.
- Handle loading states with boolean flags (`isLoading`, `isConnecting`).
- Handle error states separately from loading states.

## Code Style Preferences

- Prefer named exports over default exports (easier refactoring).
- Use destructuring for props and context values.
- Early returns for error conditions.
- Meaningful variable names (no abbreviations except standard: `id`, `url`, `ble`).

## Comments

- Comment complex BLE logic (service UUIDs, characteristic parsing).
- Comment non-obvious business logic ("why" not "what").
- Don't comment obvious code.

## When Unsure

- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask before implementing.
- If multiple approaches exist, explain trade-offs, provide breakdowsn of all options and recommend one.
- Follow these rules unless explicitly told otherwise.
